## 1. Capability 1 — asset_reload baseline: port the content-hash auto-reload to the shared display mains

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-ASSET-RELOAD] Port cyd/src/asset_reload.{h,cpp} into main.cpp and qualia_main.cpp, mirroring the already-wired controller_main.cpp

- capability: the content-hash auto-reload consumer (`cyd/src/asset_reload.h` / `.cpp`) is host-tested and already wired on `firmware/reactive_core/src/controller_main.cpp` (confirmed on read: `asset_reload_init`, `asset_reload_decide_held`, `asset_backstop_should_reload`, and the `_device/<id>/restart` fallback all present and included at controller_main.cpp lines 47/204/242/369-378/661). It is ABSENT from the shared display mains, `cyd/src/main.cpp` and `cyd/src/qualia_main.cpp` — confirmed on read: neither file includes `asset_reload.h` or calls any `asset_reload_*` function, though both already include and wire `bus_binding.h` (§2). This gap is independently named [DEFECT-CONFIG-FETCH-FIRMWARE-DOES-NOT-405602] in `asset_reload.h`'s own header comment and on [DEC-STORAGE-MERGED-DATA-ON-(a private host name, not published)-2026-09-02]'s `display-asset-reload-gap` property.
- what-porting-means: line-for-line reuse of the existing, host-tested `asset_reload.{h,cpp}` module (already proven at `env:native_test` per its own header) — this is NOT a new design, it is wiring the SAME consumer controller_main.cpp already uses onto the two remaining firmware mains, the identical shape [DEC-STORAGE-MERGED-DATA-ON-(a private host name, not published)-2026-09-02]'s `r503-not-the-exemplar` property names for the throttle controller as the right exemplar.
- self-select-rule: ⛔ **the device NEVER computes sha256.** It self-selects purely by STRING COMPARISON against a server-provided hash: `new == held` -> ignore (already latest, absorbs the retained-replay-on-connect case); `old == held` -> reload (the exact bytes I hold just changed, mine); otherwise -> not mine, do nothing (the event carries no asset path, so any other mismatch is genuinely ambiguous and must not trigger a fetch). Confirmed against `asset_reload.h`'s own documented contract, matched against `services/bus_storage/change_notify.py`'s emit shape (`{"old": <hash|null>, "new": <hash>}`, sha256 lowercase hex, computed server-side in `content_hash()`).
- sha256-claim-scope: SCOPED 2026-09-08 (architect-inference, build-diamond code read) — the `self-select-rule` above ("the device NEVER computes sha256") is TRUE of the two display-main ports this baseline targets, `cyd/src/main.cpp` and `qualia_main.cpp` (verified: neither links mbedtls/sha256; the held hash arrives via `config_fetch` reading the server's `X-Content-Hash`/`ETag` header, §3). It is FALSE of the pre-existing exemplar this node cites as already-wired, `firmware/reactive_core/src/controller_main.cpp`, which computes sha256 ON-DEVICE (`mbedtls_sha256_*`, ~line 532) and passes that locally-computed hash to `asset_reload_set_held` — a deliberate, disclosed divergence: `asset_reload_bus.h`'s own header names it "THE ONE DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE FROM controller_main.cpp." This is NOT a whole-fleet invariant; it is scoped to the display-main port. Flagged as an architect follow-on, not resolved here: either retrofit `controller_main.cpp` to the header-based self-select, or formally accept the divergence.
- auto-recovery: CORRECTED 2026-09-03 (audit resolution, Q1) — David's stated PRIMARY mechanism is DEVICE-SIDE message buffering: the device buffers inbound bus messages so a transient connectivity miss does not lose the `{old->new}` change event. This corrects an earlier draft of this node, which assumed BROKER-SIDE MQTT retention as the recovery mechanism; David clarified 2026-09-03 that caching "the inbound messages" meant the device buffering what it receives, not the broker retaining what it holds. ⛔ THIS IS AN EMPTY EVIDENCE SLOT, NOT A CLAIM: this document does not know whether device-side inbound-message buffering exists in firmware today — see [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-PRE-DEVICE-SIDE-BUFFERING] (§9) and [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-EV-10] (§10). Independently of buffering, a periodic re-hash BACKSTOP already exists in the ported module — `asset_backstop_should_reload` re-GETs the asset, hashes it, and compares to `held` on a timer and on every MQTT reconnect, closing the gap where a device holding H0 sees only the LAST transition `{H1->H2}` (a skipped multi-PUT) and correctly does nothing on the event alone. Beneath both, restart is the RECOVERY FLOOR (below).
- manual-immediate-recovery: `_device/<id>/restart` (or broadcast `_device/restart`) re-runs the EXISTING boot config-fetch path — this is the RECOVERY FLOOR: it works regardless of whether the event-driven or backstop path has landed, because it is [DEC-ESP32-REMOTE-RESTART-BUS-COMMAND]'s already-ratified, already-committed mechanism.
- recovery-floor-statement: restart-re-fetch-by-PATH is the recovery FLOOR for this whole baseline, not merely this one capability — every capability below that changes what a device holds falls back to the SAME `_device/<id>/restart` mechanism when its own live path is unproven or unavailable.
- provenance: architect-recommendation-david-agreed for the port itself (porting `asset_reload.{h,cpp}` into `main.cpp`/`qualia_main.cpp`); DAVID-RULING (retagged 2026-09-03, audit resolution) for restart-as-the-recovery-floor — this was David's ORIGINAL idea, his genesis turn: "the recovery mechanism is a restart," not an architect recommendation David merely agreed to; the underlying restart mechanism is david-ruling ([DEC-ESP32-REMOTE-RESTART-BUS-COMMAND], 2026-07-30); the underlying boot-fetch-only discipline being reacted to is architect-recommendation-david-agreed ([DEC-FETCH-ON-BOOT-CONFIG-DISCIPLINE], 2026-07-28).
- depends-on: [DEC-ESP32-REMOTE-RESTART-BUS-COMMAND]
- depends-on: [DEC-FETCH-ON-BOOT-CONFIG-DISCIPLINE]
- see-also: [CXC-ASSET-CHANGE-NOTIFY]
- see-also: [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-HASH-ON-GET]
- see-also: [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-PRE-DEVICE-SIDE-BUFFERING]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

