## 6. Capability 6 — UART / mothercard transport-flexibility

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-UART-GOAL] The goal: no reflash to enable UART on any qualifying device, mothercard or daughtercard — and a state-of-the-art correction

- goal: David's verbatim intent — no reflash to enable UART on any qualifying device, mothercard OR daughtercard. The common model: build a widget STANDALONE (riding ComposeBus directly), then LATER slave it via UART as a DAUGHTERCARD to a mothercard, zero reflash either side.
- state-of-the-art-correction: ⛔ being precise, correcting an earlier over-claim from this session — the UART ComposeBus TRANSPORT is ALREADY LIVE ON HARDWARE: an ESP32 running the Engine Thrust widget speaks ComposeBus over UART to this computer today. What is NOT yet built on hardware is (i) the MCU-as-MOTHERCARD relay and (ii) announce-bind LEVEL 3. This correction matters because it changes what this baseline needs to BUILD (the relay + L3 announce-bind) versus what it needs to CONFIGURE/EXTEND (the transport itself, already proven; the runtime-selectability of it, §6d).
- code-grounding-uart-transport: `cyd/src/bus_uart.h`'s own header confirms the transport exists and states its limits precisely: the frame codec (`bus_frame.h`) is host-parity-tested against the Python encoder (`env:native_frame`) and is shared with `firmware/ftl_jump_button/src/main.cpp` on a CH32V003; the ESP-IDF driver wraps it with a UART port, an rx task, and the same queue-to-render-task shape `bus_mqtt.cpp` uses. The header also states what is NOT done as of its authoring (2026-08-12): no upstream ComposeBus host was confirmed on a bench board's UART at that time, and `config_fetch`/`capture_http` remain WiFi-only. This baseline's own claim (Engine Thrust live on UART today) POST-DATES that header comment and should be verified fresh by the audit rather than taken from either source alone.
- header-claim-tension: UNRECONCILED 2026-09-08 (architect-inference, build-diamond code read) — `state-of-the-art-correction` above asserts the UART ComposeBus transport is ALREADY LIVE ON HARDWARE (Engine Thrust speaking ComposeBus over UART to this computer today). `bus_uart.h`'s own header comment, as of 2026-08-12, still states no upstream ComposeBus host was confirmed on a bench board's UART and that the transport "never carried a live scene." This tension is NOT resolved here — it is the architect's to close: either update `bus_uart.h`'s header comment to match the Engine-Thrust-live claim, or log the Engine-Thrust-live demo as evidence against that header's own stale negative. It does not block anything: the on-hardware proof this baseline actually needs is already the open [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-EV-06] slot, independent of which side of this tension is right.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-UART-TRANSPORT] (a) UART ComposeBus transport — frame/deframe + chunk-reassembly, DECODE not TRANSFORM

- capability: the UART transport's frame/deframe and chunk-reassembly logic is DECODE, not transform, per the ratified connector-transform boundary — [DEC-G1-CONNECTOR-TRANSFORM-BOUNDARY-2026-08-25]'s clarification (2): "DECODE-including-reassembly recovers the ORIGINAL fact and STOPS at wire-bytes; interpreting the bytes' meaning is a derive." The UART connector's job is exactly reassembling a fragmented wire payload back into `BusMessage` bytes (temptation T4 in that stress-test, explicitly held) — it must never interpret or map those bytes into a new semantic fact; that mapping, if any is needed, belongs to a state_profile peer, not the connector.
- already-proven: the codec itself is host-parity-tested (`env:native_frame`) and shared across firmware trees (`bus_frame.h`, used identically by the CH32V003 `ftl_jump_button` firmware and the ESP-IDF `bus_uart.cpp`).
- provenance: david-ruling for the UART-transport intent and its live-hardware status (Engine Thrust, stated plainly in this session); david-ratified-by-concurrence for the decode-not-transform boundary it must honor ([DEC-G1-CONNECTOR-TRANSFORM-BOUNDARY-2026-08-25], stress-tested and held 2026-08-25).
- depends-on: [DEC-G1-CONNECTOR-TRANSFORM-BOUNDARY-2026-08-25]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-RELAY-MEMBRANE] (b) The relay/mothercard forwarder enforcing the scope membrane on BOTH subscribe and publish

- capability: `cyd/src/relay_main.cpp` is prior art — a node already holding two transports (CAN upstream, UART downstream) and forwarding chunk-by-chunk between them, per [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE]'s `prior-art` property. The relay IS the membrane: [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE]'s ratified claim (2026-08-10, David) is that "a relay must respect scope on the PUBLISH path exactly as it does on the subscribe path, or a naive forward-everything relay leaks local chatter onto the shared bus" — and "the relay is NOT the scope boundary; the host's UPSTREAM transport is." A mothercard built on the relay shape without enforcing this leaks fleet-wide.
- current-hardware-status: HOST-PROVEN ONLY. `relay_main.cpp`'s own header states this precisely — "this app has been COMPILED, and config_relay's forwarding rule and TTL ladder are host-tested against the Python oracle (`env:native_config_chunk`). Whether it has carried a live transfer between two real segments depends on hardware that may not exist yet." [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE]'s `prior-art` property independently corroborates: "Host-proven only (`env:native_relay`, 40 checks) — it has never run on hardware." NO hardware run has been recorded anywhere this document depends on.
- what-must-be-built: the CAN-upstream/UART-downstream shape exists; what's missing per this baseline's scope is enforcing SCOPE on the forward path in BOTH directions (not just recording it) and proving that enforcement on real hardware — a naive port of the existing host-tested logic to a live board is not sufficient by itself if the scope check was never exercised against a genuinely local vs. genuinely global message on real silicon.
- provenance: david-ruling for the requirement that a mothercard exist at all (no-reflash-to-enable-UART, mothercard+daughtercard); architect-inference for scope-membrane being the correct enforcement mechanism, ratified separately by David 2026-08-10 on [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE] itself.
- depends-on: [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE]
- see-also: [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-SCOPE-PRECONDITION]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-ANNOUNCE-BIND-L3] (c) Daughtercard capability-announce over UART + the mothercard side that binds it — announce-bind LEVEL 3

- capability: [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]'s four-level pattern names level 3 explicitly: "A DAUGHTERCARD announces a capability descriptor to its host MOTHERCARD over the internal link (I2C or UART). The payoff is that the parent speaks ONE protocol forever: a daughtercard needing bespoke parent firmware has bought nothing." This baseline requires BOTH halves: the daughtercard-side announce, and the mothercard-side bind that consumes it.
- current-status: "NO HARDWARE," per [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]'s own `status-of-the-mechanism` property — "Level 3 has no hardware. Only level 4's widget side exists" — and its `evidence-uncountable-drop` / census evidence generally shows levels 1-2 are proposals with a task list, not running code, as of that document's own authoring. This is an EMPTY evidence slot for this baseline (§10), not a claim of readiness.
- design-check-applies: [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]'s own `design-check` property — "a mechanism built for level N MUST be reusable at N+1" — means level 3's design should reuse the SAME announce/bind shape as levels 1-2 (`_device/<id>/*` style namespacing, periodic-not-on-change publication) rather than invent a bespoke daughtercard protocol; this baseline does not relax that constraint.
- provenance: david-ruling for the no-reflash mothercard/daughtercard requirement that makes level 3 necessary; architect-inference (unratified as a standalone claim, carried forward from [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]) for the specific design-check reuse constraint applying to level 3's build.
- depends-on: [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-TRANSPORT-SELECT-RUNTIME] (d) bus_transport_select extended so MODE and UART PINS are config/NVS-selectable at runtime from board_profile

- capability: `firmware/reactive_core/src/bus_transport_select.h` is the seed — confirmed on read: it already implements the NVS-key `transport` pattern in namespace `composeio`, a PURE host-testable resolve core (`transport_select_resolve`), the loud-not-silent unknown-name rule, and the "settable over the current link, takes effect next boot" idiom the rest of this baseline reuses (matching [CXC-REPOINT]'s and [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-BUS-BINDING]'s precedence idiom). [DEC-STORAGE-MERGED-DATA-ON-(a private host name, not published)-2026-09-02]'s `r503-not-the-exemplar` property confirms this IS the R503's actual no-reflash mechanism ("Its no-reflash story is NVS transport-select"), even though the R503's endpoint reachability itself uses compile-time `#define`s for a separate reason.
- what-must-be-added: today `bus_transport_select` resolves a SUBSTRATE name (`offline`/`mqtt`/`uart`/`can`); this baseline extends it so a device additionally resolves a MODE — standalone / mothercard / daughtercard — and, for UART specifically, WHICH PINS to use, both sourced from `board_profile` (config/NVS), never from a per-build `#define`. This is what makes "build standalone, later slave as a daughtercard" a config change rather than a recompile — the literal mechanism behind the no-reflash goal in [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-UART-GOAL].
- why-nothing-per-build: the design-check in [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND] and the precedent already set by `bus_transport_select`'s own substrate-selection axis both point the same direction — a board's ROLE should be as reconfigurable as its transport already is, using the identical NVS/config idiom, not a second bespoke mechanism.
- wiring-status: WIRING-DEFERRED 2026-09-08 (architect-disposition, build-diamond: gap-analyzer + first-bringup + composeplatform-qa, independent) — `bus_transport_select.{h,cpp}` is host-complete and host-tested (mode standalone/mothercard/daughtercard + NVS UART-pin selection + the capability-predicate gate) but has ZERO firmware call sites: nothing calls `bus_transport_mode()`/`bus_transport_set_mode()`/`bus_uart_pins()` outside its own selftest. So the "build standalone, later slave as a daughtercard, zero reflash" OUTCOME cannot occur on any board yet, independent of hardware readiness. The runtime-role wiring is deferred, coupled to announce-bind L3 ([CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-ANNOUNCE-BIND-L3], itself an unmade design), and must be wired BEFORE the Phase-C comprehensive flash to preserve the flash-once promise. See decision record DEC-DEVICE-BASELINE-6D-WIRING-DEFERRED-TO-L3 (decision graph, intelligence-console corpus; cited here as plain text, not a minting edge, since it lives in a different corpus and would otherwise dangle).
- provenance: DAVID-RULING (split 2026-09-03, audit resolution) for the OUTCOME — no reflash to change a device's role; mode and UART pins are config-selectable (David's stated requirement); ARCHITECT-RECOMMENDATION for the specific MECHANISM — extending `bus_transport_select`'s existing NVS/board_profile path (identifying it as the seed to extend rather than build fresh) is the architect's proposal, not David's own mechanism choice.
- depends-on: [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-UART-GOAL]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAPABILITY-THRESHOLD] The "any device" guarantee is gated by a CAPABILITY PREDICATE evaluable from board_profile, not chip-identity — ESP32 and RP2040 are examples that clear it, the CH32V003 an example that fails it

- gate: REFRAMED 2026-09-03 per David's ruling this session — the no-reflash-to-enable-UART guarantee is gated by a CAPABILITY PREDICATE, evaluated from `board_profile`: does the device have sufficient RAM and compute to host the relay logic plus the full baseline. David's verbatim reframing: "I wanted the gate not to be based on 'is it an ESP32' but 'does the device have sufficient RAM and compute' — which it clearly does not [for the CH32V003]." The gate is NEVER chip-identity and NEVER a chip allowlist; this supersedes any earlier framing of the gate as a chip-family test.
- examples-not-allowlist: ESP32-class and the RP2040 are EXAMPLES that CLEAR the capability predicate ("an RP2040 would also surely be a qualifying MCU," David) — illustrations of devices with sufficient RAM/compute, not the definition of qualification. The CH32V003 sub-engine floor is an EXAMPLE that FAILS the predicate — insufficient RAM/compute to carry the relay + full baseline — so it is a relayed DAUGHTERCARD by construction, never a mothercard, regardless of any future firmware work on it. The exclusion is on the CAPABILITY basis, not chip-family, and is DAVID-AGREED (this session) on that basis.
- implementation-implication: a device tests its OWN capability, evaluated from its own `board_profile` — "do I have the room?" (sufficient RAM/flash to host the relay + the full baseline) — it NEVER tests "am I an ESP32." No chip-identity branch belongs anywhere in this gate's implementation.
- threshold-definition-status: defined by CAPABILITY (sufficient RAM/flash to carry the relay + the full baseline), deliberately NOT defined as a numeric RAM/flash cutoff in this document — a precise cutover number is left for the architect's audit or a follow-on decision, so this baseline does not silently over-specify a boundary David did not state numerically.
- provenance: DAVID-RULING for the capability-predicate framing itself (verbatim, 2026-09-03, correcting an earlier chip-identity framing); DAVID-AGREED (this session) for the CH32V003 exclusion specifically stated on the capability basis (insufficient RAM/compute), not chip-family; ESP32 and RP2040 are David's own exemplars of devices that clear the predicate.
- see-also: [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-UART-GOAL]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-SCOPE-PRECONDITION] Hard dependency: scope must be on the wire, or the mothercard membrane cannot enforce local-stays/global-crosses — record as a PRECONDITION TO VERIFY, not an assumption

- precondition: [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE] states this as a blocking prerequisite, not a tidiness fix — "Restoring scope to the wire is a PREREQUISITE for the mothercard architecture, not a tidiness fix. This is the one item on this page that BLOCKS a build rather than informing one." Without scope surviving a wire round-trip, [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-RELAY-MEMBRANE]'s enforcement obligation has nothing to enforce against.
- ratified-fix-claimed: BP-CAN-ID v2 (commit `dd7e049`, 2026-08-13, confirmed on read: "arbitration gets a DESIGN, scope gets a field, the value gets a type," layout `prio(3) | type(4) | ns_id(16) | src(4) | scope(2) = 29` bits, ratified by David after an independent CAN specialist's teardown) added the scope field and is stated in this baseline's brief to have ENDED the CAN-layout divergence between `python/composeio/transport_can.py` and `services/can_mqtt_bridge`.
- discrepancy-flagged-not-resolved: ⛔ VERIFIED 2026-09-03 — RESOLVED, discrepancy closed. This document did not take the "ended" framing on faith; the flag stood until the code itself was read on 2026-09-03 against all three wire sites: `cyd/src/bus_can_id.h` (`bus_can_id_encode`, shifts `kCanPrioShift=26/kCanTypeShift=22/kCanNsShift=6/kCanSrcShift=2/kCanScopeShift=0`), `python/composeio/transport_can.py` (`encode_arbitration_id`/`decode_arbitration_id`, lines 171-207, identical shift/mask constants) and `services/can_mqtt_bridge/bridge.py` (lines 602-672, which IMPORTS `encode_arbitration_id`/`decode_arbitration_id`/`can_id_ns_hash` directly from `composeio.transport_can` rather than reimplementing the codec — so bridge and transport share one codec, not two that happen to agree). All three speak `prio(3)|type(4)|ns_id(16)|src(4)|scope(2)` identically; grep across all three for the old layouts (`ns_hash(16)|type(4)|src(9)`, `type(4)|scope(2)|ns(16)|seq(7)`) turns up only historical comments documenting the fix, no live code. The two corpus records this property used to flag as contradicting "ended" have both now been synced to match: [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE]'s `current-defect`/`defect-is-not-free-to-fix` properties in `62-composeplatform-vision.lts` and [DEC-CAN-ARBITRATION-ID-LAYOUT-DIVERGENCE]'s `lifecycle-position` (now `resolved`) in the decision graph.
- what-this-means-for-the-baseline: the UART transport ([CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-UART-TRANSPORT]) and the relay/mothercard membrane ([CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-RELAY-MEMBRANE]) MUST use the SAME `BusMessage` scope-carrying encoding `dd7e049` establishes — that requirement is not in doubt, and it is now also VERIFIED that `CANTransport` and `can_mqtt_bridge` are BOTH reconciled onto `dd7e049`'s layout in code, sharing the same codec module. This precondition is MET at [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-PRE-SCOPE-WIRE-VERIFY] (§9) — see that node for the verdict. What remains unverified is only the ON-HARDWARE proof at [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-EV-06]/[CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-EV-07], which this code-read does not substitute for.
- provenance: david-ruling for the hard dependency itself (scope-on-wire is a prerequisite, stated in this session's brief) and for the BP-CAN-ID v2 ratification (`dd7e049`, David, 2026-08-13); architect-inference for the discrepancy flag and its 2026-09-03 resolution, both this document's own finding from reading the corpus and code, not claims attributed to David.
- depends-on: [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE]
- see-also: [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-PRE-SCOPE-WIRE-VERIFY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

