## 7. Explicit non-goals — recorded so they are not silently reintroduced

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-NONGOAL-REHASH] On-device integrity re-hashing is explicitly NOT in this baseline

- non-goal: the device does not re-hash a fetched asset to verify its own integrity. The transport already guarantees corruption-integrity on the trusted LAN, and re-hashing on a constrained MCU is compute deliberately avoided.
- provenance: david-ruling.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-NONGOAL-SOFT-RELOAD] Soft (in-place, no-reboot) config reload is explicitly NOT USB-flash-motivated and is out unless a specific case arises

- non-goal: both `_device/<id>/restart` (hard reboot) and a hypothetical soft in-place reload are already USB-free, so a soft reload buys nothing toward THIS baseline's driver (eliminating USB visits) — it would only shave reboot flicker. Optional, per-widget, ONLY if a specific reboot-flicker case arises later; not part of this comprehensive pass.
- provenance: architect-recommendation-david-agreed.
- see-also: [DEC-ESP32-REMOTE-RESTART-BUS-COMMAND]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-NONGOAL-OTA] OTA is intentionally out of scope now — the eventual real answer, named as the horizon

- non-goal: full ESP32/RP2040 OTA firmware-update capability is a separate, bigger decision, already named as a follow-on by [DEC-ESP32-REMOTE-RESTART-BUS-COMMAND]'s own `follow-on` property ("a full ESP32 OTA firmware-update capability... fixes the firmware-CAPABILITY case this restart cannot"). This baseline exists BECAUSE OTA is not being built now — bundling every confident capability into one flash pass is the mitigation, not the cure.
- provenance: david-ruling (OTA intentionally not supported now; recorded as the horizon).
- see-also: [DEC-DEVICE-BASELINE-FLASH-ONCE-THEN-CONFIG]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

---

## 8. Deferred with trigger — fetch-by-hash / content-addressed store

### [DD-DEVICE-BASELINE-DEFER-FETCH-BY-HASH] Defer full fetch-by-hash / CAS + [CXC-ACN-RECOVERY] + [CXC-ACN-DELTA] — bus_storage is PATH-addressed today, verified

- decision: DEFER building fetch-by-hash / a content-addressed store, [CXC-ACN-RECOVERY] (hash->current lineage index), and [CXC-ACN-DELTA] (delta/patch transfer). NOT in this baseline.
- lifecycle-position: decided
- status: decided
- verification: bus_storage is PATH-addressed today — confirmed on read of `services/bus_storage/server.py`'s routing table: every GET route (`/api/v1/assets/<path>`, `/api/v1/blob/<path>`, `/api/v1/configs/<id>`) resolves by path/name/id; there is NO by-hash GET route anywhere in the file. Hashes are used ONLY for change-detection (`content_hash()` inside `AssetChangeNotifier`), never as an address.
- resolution: the device stores `{path, server-provided-hash}` per [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-HASH-ON-GET] / [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-ASSET-RELOAD]; PATH is the recovery floor via `_device/<id>/restart`'s boot-fetch-by-path.
- invalidating_assumption: this holds while (a) no consumer needs delta/patch transfer, and (b) fleet scale / shared-asset dedup does not make content-addressing pay for itself. The TRIGGER to build fetch-by-hash/CAS is either: adoption of delta-transfer as a requirement, OR fleet scale/dedup pressure making a by-hash address economical. Either condition reopens this decision.
- rationale: bus_storage's existing path-addressed shape already covers everything this baseline needs (self-select by hash comparison, not by-hash fetch); building CAS now would be machinery ahead of a proven need, the same discipline [DD-STORE-QUERY-DISPOSITION] applied to the server-side query engine.
- alternatives: build fetch-by-hash/CAS now, ahead of a proven need — rejected because bus_storage's path-addressed shape already covers this baseline's needs (self-select by hash comparison, never by-hash fetch), so the machinery would sit unused, the same shape [DD-STORE-QUERY-DISPOSITION]'s own `alternatives` property rejected for the server-side query engine.
- provenance: DAVID-RULING (split 2026-09-03, audit resolution) for the DEFERRAL itself — David agreed to defer; ARCHITECT-RECOMMENDATION for the specific TRIGGER CONDITIONS — the architect's analysis of when delta-transfer adoption or fleet-scale/dedup pressure would reopen this decision; architect-recommendation for the verification method (reading the live route table rather than trusting a prior claim).
- closes-question: this deferral CLOSES David's original [CXC-ACN-RECOVERY] usefulness-in-parallel question — David confirmed 2026-09-03: "Yes, 'defer fetch-by-hash' also closes my [CXC-ACN-RECOVERY] question." [CXC-ACN-RECOVERY] (device-pull recovery against a content-lineage index) is deferred/settled: recovery-via-lineage needs fetch-by-hash, which is deferred here.
- see-also: [CXC-ASSET-CHANGE-NOTIFY]
- see-also: [CXC-ACN-RECOVERY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

