## 9. Pre-integration questions — for the design-audit and for the bench pass

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-PRE-BUS-BINDING-BENCH] Has the bus_binding repoint/confirm/revert loop been proven on real silicon?

- question: EMPTY. Per [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-BUS-BINDING], the state machine is host-proven but the live repoint, the auto-revert window, and the two-stage escape-hatch GPIO have not been bench-confirmed on any of the 8 boards. This baseline's comprehensive flash pass is a candidate moment to close this gap, but it has not yet been run.
- see-also: [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-BUS-BINDING]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-PRE-RELAY-HARDWARE] Has the CAN-up/UART-down relay ever carried a live transfer between two real segments?

- question: EMPTY. `relay_main.cpp` is compiled and host-tested only; no record in any corpus document this baseline depends on shows it running on hardware.
- see-also: [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-RELAY-MEMBRANE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-PRE-ANNOUNCE-BIND-HARDWARE] Has announce-bind level 3 (daughtercard-to-mothercard) run on any hardware?

- question: EMPTY. [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND] states plainly that level 3 has no hardware.
- see-also: [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-ANNOUNCE-BIND-L3]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-PRE-DOC-PURGE-CENSUS] Beyond the 2 known app-surface refs, has a census found every remaining doc:// reference reachable from device-served config or live-store docs?

- question: EMPTY. No census beyond the 2 known app-surface refs (`fleet-540-soak`, `minnow-bringup`) has been run against device-served configs or the live merged store.
- see-also: [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-DOC-PURGE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-PRE-DEVICE-SIDE-BUFFERING] Does device-side inbound-message buffering exist in firmware today?

- question: EMPTY. Per [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-ASSET-RELOAD]'s `auto-recovery` property (corrected 2026-09-03, audit resolution Q1), David's stated PRIMARY miss-recovery mechanism is DEVICE-SIDE buffering of inbound bus messages — NOT broker-side MQTT retention, which an earlier draft of this document wrongly assumed. This document does not know whether such buffering exists in any device firmware today; it is recorded here as David's stated mechanism to VERIFY or BUILD, not as an already-present capability.
- see-also: [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-CAP-ASSET-RELOAD]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

### [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-PRE-SCOPE-WIRE-VERIFY] Does `dd7e049` actually reconcile the CANTransport / can_mqtt_bridge layout divergence, or only the spec side?

- question: VERIFIED 2026-09-03 — MET, not EMPTY. Read `cyd/src/bus_can_id.h` (`bus_can_id_encode`, `kCanPrioShift=26/kCanTypeShift=22/kCanNsShift=6/kCanSrcShift=2/kCanScopeShift=0`), `python/composeio/transport_can.py` (`encode_arbitration_id`/`decode_arbitration_id`, lines 171-207) and `services/can_mqtt_bridge/bridge.py` (lines 602-672) directly. All three implement `prio(3)|type(4)|ns_id(16)|src(4)|scope(2)` identically, and `bridge.py` does not reimplement the codec — it `from composeio.transport_can import (encode_arbitration_id, decode_arbitration_id, can_id_ns_hash, ...)` at line 201, so the bridge and the Python transport are provably the SAME codec, not two independent implementations that happen to agree. A grep for both retired layouts (`ns_hash(16)|type(4)|src(9)` and `type(4)|scope(2)|ns(16)|seq(7)`) across all three files finds them only in comments narrating the fix, never in live code. The divergence [DEC-CAN-ARBITRATION-ID-LAYOUT-DIVERGENCE] described is CLOSED in code, and the two corpus records [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-SCOPE-PRECONDITION]'s `discrepancy-flagged-not-resolved` property named as still contradicting "ended" ([CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE], [DEC-CAN-ARBITRATION-ID-LAYOUT-DIVERGENCE]) have been synced to match this verdict. [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-RELAY-MEMBRANE] and [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-UART-TRANSPORT] may now be built against a settled wire format; only their ON-HARDWARE evidence ([CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-EV-06], [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-EV-07]) remains open, and this verification does not stand in for it.
- see-also: [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-SCOPE-PRECONDITION]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE]

